WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:06.610
- Good afternoon.

00:00:06.610 --> 00:00:07.760
Here we are.

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:10.820
It's been an incredible few days,

00:00:10.820 --> 00:00:13.173
and there's still lots more to come.

00:00:14.740 --> 00:00:19.273
This weekend we've
remembered, we've talked,

00:00:20.310 --> 00:00:23.830
we've debated, we've laughed.

00:00:23.830 --> 00:00:25.513
Some of us have cried.

00:00:27.010 --> 00:00:30.380
This final programmatic
salute to the first

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:32.900
women in Yale College includes

00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:35.723
two different kinds of segments.

00:00:36.630 --> 00:00:39.630
One segment includes short videos

00:00:39.630 --> 00:00:43.527
created by Kyle Gibson Class of 1978

00:00:45.650 --> 00:00:48.940
from the wonderful oral histories

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:53.940
taken by some of you,
or given by some of you,

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:56.763
during the course of the last few years.

00:00:57.860 --> 00:01:00.890
The oral history includes

00:01:00.890 --> 00:01:04.840
many more deeply compelling interviews,

00:01:04.840 --> 00:01:09.100
but these brief excerpts capture

00:01:09.100 --> 00:01:12.420
a fraction of your brilliant voices

00:01:12.420 --> 00:01:17.420
and touch on some themes
common to that first wild year,

00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:21.780
especially the challenges, the hurdles,

00:01:21.780 --> 00:01:25.710
and the surprises that
came with being a part

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.483
of that tumultuous, historic time.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.970
The rest of the program
is music from our era,

00:01:33.970 --> 00:01:38.580
some of it performed either
by groups or members of groups

00:01:38.580 --> 00:01:41.410
that were founded during our era,

00:01:41.410 --> 00:01:44.130
some of it by more current groups

00:01:44.130 --> 00:01:48.213
selected because they sing
music that is relevant.

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:51.660
We felt that music from our era would be

00:01:51.660 --> 00:01:54.910
good background for our memories,

00:01:54.910 --> 00:01:57.170
and we also felt that having the music

00:01:57.170 --> 00:02:02.170
sung by today's undergraduates,
most of them women,

00:02:02.290 --> 00:02:05.110
would be inspiring since they are

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.743
the triumphant outcome of what we began.

00:02:09.990 --> 00:02:12.760
Along the way, I, Connie Royster,

00:02:12.760 --> 00:02:16.710
and Shelley Fisher
Fishkin, and Julia Preston

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.470
will provide some brief narration

00:02:19.470 --> 00:02:23.053
to introduce, guide, and explain.

00:02:24.140 --> 00:02:29.140
So, to begin, our first group
will be the Slavic chorus.

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:30.914
- [Audience Member] Yay.

00:02:30.914 --> 00:02:32.244
(Connie laughing)

00:02:32.244 --> 00:02:35.610
(audience applauding)

00:02:35.610 --> 00:02:39.430
- Founded in 1969, the first year

00:02:39.430 --> 00:02:42.270
of undergraduate co-education at Yale.

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.970
And I know there are members

00:02:43.970 --> 00:02:46.480
of the Slavic chorus here in the audience.

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:51.143
Would you please stand up
and wave so we can see you.

00:02:51.143 --> 00:02:55.393
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.360
The Slavs have been wildly praised,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:11.910
not only for their sound
but for their authenticity.

00:03:11.910 --> 00:03:15.790
In 2019 they celebrated
their 50th anniversity

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.900
with a reunion concert that drew alumni

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:21.160
from across the globe.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:25.670
Without further adieu, I
give you today's Slavs,

00:03:25.670 --> 00:03:28.310
a chorus that has always been dedicated

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:30.910
to maintaining musical excellence

00:03:30.910 --> 00:03:34.890
and seeking to empower women through song.

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:37.630
They will be singing one
of their earliest songs

00:03:37.630 --> 00:03:42.630
from our era, "Erghen
Deda," a song from Bulgaria.

00:03:45.462 --> 00:03:48.629
(audience applauding)

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:12.383
(chorus singing in a foreign language)

00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:12.146
(chorus members clapping in time)

00:06:33.061 --> 00:06:37.228
(chorus members clapping in time)

00:07:07.682 --> 00:07:11.932
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:07:24.208 --> 00:07:26.290
- What a wonderful performance.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.273
Thank you, Slavs.

00:07:29.870 --> 00:07:33.370
The New Blue was also founded in 1969,

00:07:33.370 --> 00:07:37.500
another of the very first of
Yale women's singing groups.

00:07:37.500 --> 00:07:40.110
In that year, Ben O'Keefe, Bill Harwood,

00:07:40.110 --> 00:07:42.160
and Jim Weber of the Yale Glee Club

00:07:42.160 --> 00:07:44.700
held the first auditions for a brand new,

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:47.240
all women's a cappella singing group.

00:07:47.240 --> 00:07:50.550
The 11 women who auditioned
were accepted into the group.

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:53.420
With a pitch and a
business manager assigned

00:07:53.420 --> 00:07:55.660
and a single arrangement from Feno,

00:07:55.660 --> 00:07:57.610
the women were on their own.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:01.750
These dynamic women soon
dubbed themselves The New Blue,

00:08:01.750 --> 00:08:04.630
a name representing a new face of Yale,

00:08:04.630 --> 00:08:07.970
recasting the mold of Yale's Old Blue.

00:08:07.970 --> 00:08:10.520
At first, not surprising to us,

00:08:10.520 --> 00:08:14.460
it was difficult for The New
Blue to be taken seriously.

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:17.457
When a member of a male
a cappella group quipped,

00:08:17.457 --> 00:08:20.320
"The New Who?", The New Blue decided

00:08:20.320 --> 00:08:23.771
to entitle their first
album "Since You Asked."

00:08:23.771 --> 00:08:25.250
(audience laughing)

00:08:25.250 --> 00:08:28.920
Today, The New Blue is a
core part of the Yale scene.

00:08:28.920 --> 00:08:30.970
They have delighted audiences worldwide

00:08:30.970 --> 00:08:32.550
and have sung for former Presidents

00:08:32.550 --> 00:08:34.660
George Bush and Barack Obama.

00:08:34.660 --> 00:08:35.920
And I think there's some members

00:08:35.920 --> 00:08:37.310
of The New Blue in the audience.

00:08:37.310 --> 00:08:39.954
Would you please stand and wave.

00:08:39.954 --> 00:08:41.641
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:08:41.641 --> 00:08:42.474
Woo.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:52.830
I now give you the inimitable New Blue

00:08:52.830 --> 00:08:54.270
celebrating what I hope is our

00:08:54.270 --> 00:08:56.973
present frame of mind, "Feeling Good".

00:08:58.543 --> 00:09:01.710
(audience applauding)

