♪ Whenever we remember ♪

00:18:00.595 --> 00:18:05.595
♪ The way we were ♪

00:18:07.465 --> 00:18:10.298
♪ The way we were ♪

00:18:19.316 --> 00:18:23.566
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:18:39.375 --> 00:18:42.542
(audience chattering)

00:18:47.270 --> 00:18:49.740
- It appealed to my sense of history,

00:18:49.740 --> 00:18:53.510
my sense of wanting to achieve and strive.

00:18:53.510 --> 00:18:56.320
And I loved the idea of being able to be

00:18:56.320 --> 00:18:59.080
in the first class of something
to make something work.

00:18:59.080 --> 00:19:01.870
- Women were sort of
devalued in my family,

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:03.673
significantly devalued.

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:07.780
I mean, I remember when my parents

00:19:07.780 --> 00:19:10.460
would have the extended family over,

00:19:10.460 --> 00:19:12.936
and I wanted to be in the den

00:19:12.936 --> 00:19:15.780
with the men talking
about law and politics.

00:19:15.780 --> 00:19:17.080
My mother would say, "No, no,

00:19:17.080 --> 00:19:19.727
"you need to come in the kitchen with us

00:19:19.727 --> 00:19:21.500
"and talk about what we talk about."

00:19:21.500 --> 00:19:23.050
Which was not law and politics.

00:19:24.300 --> 00:19:25.500
And that's just one example.

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:26.903
There was so much of that.

00:19:28.640 --> 00:19:30.670
And so, I just thought, well, I guess

00:19:30.670 --> 00:19:33.050
the way to be worthwhile in life

00:19:33.050 --> 00:19:37.740
is to be more like a man, to
have masculine characteristics.

00:19:37.740 --> 00:19:40.753
And Yale would be a great
place to sort of find that.

00:19:40.753 --> 00:19:42.547
(audience laughing)

00:19:42.547 --> 00:19:44.820
- I grew up in New Haven.

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:49.820
I was born in New Haven, just
two blocks behind the gym,

00:19:50.910 --> 00:19:52.040
Payne Whitney Gym.

00:19:52.040 --> 00:19:55.530
My Aunt Edna Carnegie said to me

00:19:55.530 --> 00:19:59.690
that my grandfather would be turning over

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:04.690
in his grave knowing
that his granddaughter

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:06.843
had been admitted to Yale.

00:20:11.120 --> 00:20:15.360
- And when I arrived at the campus,

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:18.430
all the stores had welcome students signs,

00:20:18.430 --> 00:20:21.259
and one said, "Welcome Yalettes."

00:20:21.259 --> 00:20:22.503
(audience laughing)

00:20:22.503 --> 00:20:24.770
Which just made me cringe.

00:20:24.770 --> 00:20:27.060
- My parents took me in the station wagon,

00:20:27.060 --> 00:20:29.150
and there were a lot of reporters around

00:20:29.150 --> 00:20:30.590
asking me questions.

00:20:30.590 --> 00:20:34.550
And I do remember being
absolutely clueless

00:20:34.550 --> 00:20:36.500
as to what to say to these people.

00:20:36.500 --> 00:20:39.720
- There was so much media
coverage it was embarrassing.

00:20:39.720 --> 00:20:42.400
I remember being, I
think, on the front page

00:20:42.400 --> 00:20:44.370
with a couple of other local girls.

00:20:44.370 --> 00:20:46.390
I did not want that, and they had also

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:50.530
taken a lot of our comments
on phone interviews

00:20:50.530 --> 00:20:53.100
completely out of context, made us sound

00:20:53.100 --> 00:20:57.340
like the most entitled
(laughing), privileged,

00:20:57.340 --> 00:21:00.380
upper class terrible people.

00:21:00.380 --> 00:21:03.740
- If I could go back and talk

00:21:03.740 --> 00:21:05.583
to the young Betsy going to Yale,

00:21:06.610 --> 00:21:10.067
I would say, "You are brave.

00:21:10.067 --> 00:21:12.087
"You are a pioneer.

00:21:12.087 --> 00:21:16.010
"You are doing something really amazing."

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:21.010
- And it was the first time I
really felt I could be myself.

00:21:21.560 --> 00:21:25.350
Not only that, but the place
was full of smart people.

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:28.030
I was no longer the odd person out.

00:21:28.030 --> 00:21:32.100
It was the first time in
my life I felt normal.

00:21:32.100 --> 00:21:34.543
- I think I was in heaven (laughing).

00:21:34.543 --> 00:21:37.320
I mean, it was, you
know, it was very funny.

00:21:37.320 --> 00:21:42.200
I, many years later, a
woman who was my roommate

00:21:42.200 --> 00:21:45.693
junior year who had also
transferred from Smith with me,

00:21:46.810 --> 00:21:48.720
we had gone to a Yale reunion,

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:50.630
maybe it was our 20th
reunion or something.

00:21:50.630 --> 00:21:54.250
And she told she had just
come back from a panel

00:21:54.250 --> 00:21:56.670
of women who had graduated in the '80s,

00:21:56.670 --> 00:21:59.200
who were all complaining
that we didn't have this,

00:21:59.200 --> 00:22:01.013
and we didn't have that.

00:22:01.013 --> 00:22:02.997
And I remember Susan
looked at me, and she said,

00:22:02.997 --> 00:22:05.527
"It never occurred to us to complain.

00:22:05.527 --> 00:22:09.360
"We were so damn grateful
to just be there."

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:13.170
- So, I think in my class
there were 100 women,

00:22:13.170 --> 00:22:16.720
and there were, as Kingman
Brewster proudly told us

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:19.250
on the first day a thousand
male leaders, right?

00:22:19.250 --> 00:22:22.960
So, the ratio was extreme.

00:22:22.960 --> 00:22:24.920
- And they were welcoming us.

00:22:24.920 --> 00:22:28.443
And the first thing that of
course the staff says is,

00:22:29.697 --> 00:22:33.087
"The mission of Yale College is

00:22:33.087 --> 00:22:37.613
"to shape a thousand male leaders

00:22:37.613 --> 00:22:39.110
"(chuckling) in each class."

00:22:39.110 --> 00:22:41.300
and from the back of the common room,

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:44.117
there was a female voice
that piped up saying,

00:22:44.117 --> 00:22:46.384
"What about us?"

00:22:46.384 --> 00:22:49.510
And I always remember
the laughter after that

00:22:49.510 --> 00:22:53.040
because no mention was made about

00:22:53.040 --> 00:22:56.100
this is a historic moment,
the first class of women.

00:22:56.100 --> 00:22:58.713
They basically pretended
that we weren't there.

00:23:02.370 --> 00:23:04.510
- I don't think it really occurred to me

00:23:04.510 --> 00:23:06.210
what it was gonna feel
like in the classroom

00:23:06.210 --> 00:23:08.840
where there were both male
professors and male students

00:23:08.840 --> 00:23:11.000
who really wished we weren't there.

00:23:11.000 --> 00:23:14.400
- There were 17 men in
the major and myself.

00:23:14.400 --> 00:23:18.263
And when I arrived, I had
on a very colorful outfit,

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:22.923
and the professor, who was British,

00:23:23.800 --> 00:23:26.350
said, "You must be in the wrong place."

00:23:26.350 --> 00:23:27.183
(audience laughing)

00:23:27.183 --> 00:23:29.130
I said, "Is this MB&amp;B 302?"

00:23:29.130 --> 00:23:29.963
Or whatever.

00:23:29.963 --> 00:23:31.230
And he said, "Yes."

00:23:31.230 --> 00:23:34.270
I said, "Well, then I'm in the major."

00:23:34.270 --> 00:23:37.310
And he was stunned, and
he looked on the list.

00:23:37.310 --> 00:23:39.940
He asked my name, and
there I was on the list.

00:23:39.940 --> 00:23:41.867
And he said, "Well, Miss Gluckin will add

00:23:41.867 --> 00:23:46.053
"lovely colors and nice
sense to the class."

00:23:46.053 --> 00:23:47.330
(audience laughing)

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:49.020
I was incensed.

00:23:49.020 --> 00:23:51.600
I was furious.

00:23:51.600 --> 00:23:55.320
So, I studied super hard,
and on the first quiz,

00:23:55.320 --> 00:23:58.640
I did better than anyone else on the quiz.

00:23:58.640 --> 00:24:01.540
They were handing, the
results were handed out,

00:24:01.540 --> 00:24:02.870
and he recanted.

00:24:02.870 --> 00:24:05.453
- I felt very competitive, like I could,

00:24:06.570 --> 00:24:08.910
not blend in but that I could fit in.

00:24:08.910 --> 00:24:11.610
And I think there's a
big difference between,

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:13.980
because I didn't feel like
I had to give up anything.

00:24:13.980 --> 00:24:17.780
I felt that, and I also
felt welcomed by the

00:24:17.780 --> 00:24:21.520
white male students
who were in my classes.

00:24:21.520 --> 00:24:23.490
So, I was going in as a junior.

00:24:23.490 --> 00:24:27.050
I was going into advanced
psychology courses so I,

00:24:27.050 --> 00:24:29.620
and the only thing that
was sometimes awkward

00:24:29.620 --> 00:24:32.020
is if sometimes they looked for me

00:24:32.020 --> 00:24:34.970
to the female point of view
or to the black point of view.

00:24:34.970 --> 00:24:36.180
And I can't speak for all women.

00:24:36.180 --> 00:24:38.080
I can't speak for all black people.

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:42.780
But at least I felt like I was

00:24:44.070 --> 00:24:47.110
not being, not getting pushback.

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:49.360
- I was my freshmen year, and it was

00:24:49.360 --> 00:24:53.870
in direct studies in my English class,

00:24:53.870 --> 00:24:56.340
where I had been getting A's on my papers

00:24:56.340 --> 00:24:57.690
that you write a paper every week.

00:24:57.690 --> 00:25:01.220
And then, all of a sudden
I got a C on a paper,

00:25:01.220 --> 00:25:04.120
and the note said, "See me."

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:06.100
So, I had to go to the office of the guy.

00:25:06.100 --> 00:25:08.660
I get to the office, and it turned out

00:25:08.660 --> 00:25:12.420
that he made a pass at me,
chased me around the room,

00:25:12.420 --> 00:25:14.160
tried to lock me in the room.

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:16.010
It was really appalling.

00:25:16.010 --> 00:25:20.400
And luckily, I'm from the West
Coast in a co-ed environment.

00:25:20.400 --> 00:25:22.810
And I looked at him and
like, "Are you serious?"

00:25:22.810 --> 00:25:25.007
That's what, (laughing)
I wasn't frightened.

00:25:25.007 --> 00:25:29.040
I was just like appalled
that this was happening.

00:25:29.040 --> 00:25:32.140
So, I found a way to get out of there.

00:25:32.140 --> 00:25:35.110
And then, you think it would be over.

00:25:35.110 --> 00:25:36.170
I told my roommates.

00:25:36.170 --> 00:25:37.120
I didn't report it.

00:25:38.030 --> 00:25:41.820
And then, the next week,
I write another paper.

00:25:41.820 --> 00:25:44.817
I got a D on the paper and a note saying,

00:25:44.817 --> 00:25:47.590
"This is the best paper
you've written all year."

00:25:47.590 --> 00:25:50.090
I'm thinking, this guy is an idiot.

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:52.380
So, the next week, I think I might've

00:25:52.380 --> 00:25:54.230
even gotten an F on one.

00:25:54.230 --> 00:25:56.220
And my roommates were like,
"You have to report him."

00:25:56.220 --> 00:25:57.741
And I said, "Oh, no."

00:25:57.741 --> 00:26:00.170
If he proceeds with this,

00:26:00.170 --> 00:26:01.980
I don't wanna wreck anyone's career.

00:26:01.980 --> 00:26:03.690
If he persists in wanting me

00:26:03.690 --> 00:26:06.023
to go to his office, I won't go.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:10.930
And finals day comes, and
they pass out the blue books.

00:26:10.930 --> 00:26:14.340
And I sat in the front
row, and this teacher

00:26:14.340 --> 00:26:17.540
got up to the podium and gave
the instructions for the exam.

00:26:17.540 --> 00:26:20.130
And before he left for the exam,

00:26:20.130 --> 00:26:24.350
I got up and went up to
the podium with my back

00:26:24.350 --> 00:26:27.590
to everybody so they couldn't
tell what I was saying.

00:26:27.590 --> 00:26:30.207
And I said, "Excuse me, sir.

00:26:30.207 --> 00:26:32.857
"Shall I take the exam, or
are you going to fail me?"

00:26:34.100 --> 00:26:39.100
And he looked at me, and
said, "Take the exam."

00:26:39.230 --> 00:26:43.543
So, I took the exam, and I
got a good grade in the class.

00:26:43.543 --> 00:26:46.237
And I think I still have
those papers (laughing).

00:26:47.610 --> 00:26:49.900
Because it was to me, you know,

00:26:49.900 --> 00:26:52.300
it wasn't until later that I realized

00:26:52.300 --> 00:26:54.140
that this kind of person might be doing

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:56.983
this to someone who was more vulnerable,

00:26:58.150 --> 00:26:59.983
but it was a strange time.

00:27:05.670 --> 00:27:08.070
- This was the first year.

00:27:08.070 --> 00:27:10.190
There was nothing there, I mean,

00:27:10.190 --> 00:27:14.650
even for women who were huge
athletes, which I wasn't.

00:27:14.650 --> 00:27:17.180
But for women who were, there were no,

00:27:17.180 --> 00:27:19.350
you know, there were no
organized sports teams.

00:27:19.350 --> 00:27:22.270
- It was a bit of a shock to me to come in

00:27:22.270 --> 00:27:24.870
and ask where I could sign
up for the field hockey team

00:27:24.870 --> 00:27:26.800
and have the gentleman behind the desk

00:27:26.800 --> 00:27:29.500
look at me like I was
asking to go to Mars.

00:27:29.500 --> 00:27:33.623
He just looked at me
blankly, like, what's that?

00:27:35.120 --> 00:27:38.470
So, that was discouraging, but, you know,

00:27:38.470 --> 00:27:41.080
like so many of the women
in that first class,

00:27:41.080 --> 00:27:44.330
I think, probably we were here in part

00:27:44.330 --> 00:27:46.710
because we were resilient creatures.

00:27:46.710 --> 00:27:49.200
And so we decided to go ahead.

00:27:49.200 --> 00:27:50.340
If there wasn't a field hockey team,

00:27:50.340 --> 00:27:52.010
we would start one,
and that's what we did.

00:27:52.010 --> 00:27:53.960
- There were so few Asian Americans,

00:27:53.960 --> 00:27:57.100
and I didn't think that it was unusual

00:27:57.100 --> 00:28:00.410
to have Asian Americans, having
grown up in part in Hawaii.

00:28:00.410 --> 00:28:03.300
But to see how isolated we were.

00:28:03.300 --> 00:28:05.520
So, I started working with a number

00:28:05.520 --> 00:28:06.790
of other Asian Americans.

00:28:06.790 --> 00:28:08.810
We ended up co-founding something called

00:28:08.810 --> 00:28:10.100
the Asian American, it's now called

00:28:10.100 --> 00:28:11.920
the Asian American Student Alliance,

00:28:11.920 --> 00:28:14.803
which just celebrated
its 50th anniversary.

00:28:17.320 --> 00:28:18.750
- There was still the phenomenon

00:28:18.750 --> 00:28:21.600
of Friday afternoon
buses arriving on campus

00:28:21.600 --> 00:28:23.560
with young women from other schools

00:28:23.560 --> 00:28:24.810
who were coming for the weekend

00:28:24.810 --> 00:28:28.240
with their hat boxes or
suitcases or what have you

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:30.760
dressed in skirts, that
I remember distinctly

00:28:30.760 --> 00:28:33.220
while most of us were dressed in jeans.

00:28:33.220 --> 00:28:34.800
And realizing that, you know, the guys

00:28:34.800 --> 00:28:36.840
who were your friends during the week

00:28:36.840 --> 00:28:38.870
now were, you know, having a

00:28:38.870 --> 00:28:41.880
social experience with other women.

00:28:41.880 --> 00:28:43.870
You know, that you didn't
count in that category.

00:28:43.870 --> 00:28:46.950
- I was active in the student
government of my college,

00:28:46.950 --> 00:28:47.880
and that was one the things I'm like

00:28:47.880 --> 00:28:50.210
we're not spending any money bringing

00:28:50.210 --> 00:28:52.270
women into the college, you know?

00:28:52.270 --> 00:28:54.580
Let their college pay for the buses.

00:28:54.580 --> 00:28:56.950
We're not paying for
buses for women any more.

00:28:56.950 --> 00:28:58.870
And people thought that was horrible,

00:28:58.870 --> 00:29:00.790
but, you know, it's like, why should

00:29:00.790 --> 00:29:02.880
my money go to bus these girls in

00:29:02.880 --> 00:29:04.801
when I don't have a date, you know?

00:29:04.801 --> 00:29:06.153
(audience laughing)

00:29:06.153 --> 00:29:07.423
- It was kind of hard to avoid.

00:29:09.690 --> 00:29:12.350
You know, as soon as you decided

00:29:12.350 --> 00:29:15.233
you didn't enjoy this one, another one

00:29:15.233 --> 00:29:17.400
or two or three showed up.

00:29:17.400 --> 00:29:19.270
- I set a rule for myself freshman year

00:29:19.270 --> 00:29:22.082
that I would only got
out on four dates a week.

00:29:22.082 --> 00:29:22.953
(audience member laughing)

00:29:22.953 --> 00:29:24.579
And I stuck to it.

00:29:24.579 --> 00:29:25.803
And (laughing) it was, I had a lot of fun.

00:29:30.320 --> 00:29:32.560
- I had a song written to me that was

00:29:32.560 --> 00:29:34.860
sung every time I entered the

00:29:34.860 --> 00:29:37.130
dining hall at Pearson College.

00:29:37.130 --> 00:29:41.820
The song was written by a
man named Don Tenneblatt.

00:29:41.820 --> 00:29:43.410
He would sit at the grand piano

00:29:43.410 --> 00:29:45.810
in the lobby of Pearson College,

00:29:45.810 --> 00:29:48.460
and when I would come in
for dinner he would sing it.

00:29:49.673 --> 00:29:54.346
♪ I'm from Little Rock, Arkansas ♪

00:29:54.346 --> 00:29:58.817
♪ I'm happy that I got to know y'all ♪

00:29:58.817 --> 00:30:00.657
♪ I'm pretty as sin ♪

00:30:00.657 --> 00:30:03.361
♪ But I won't let you in ♪

00:30:03.361 --> 00:30:07.882
♪ I'm hot shit, but
I'll never show y'all ♪

00:30:07.882 --> 00:30:08.715
(audience laughing)

00:30:08.715 --> 00:30:09.914
That's my song.

00:30:09.914 --> 00:30:11.199
(audience laughing)

00:30:11.199 --> 00:30:14.783
And so, that was something I
had never experienced before.

00:30:15.620 --> 00:30:18.900
I can't say that it was a treat

00:30:18.900 --> 00:30:20.953
for me to hear that repeatedly.

00:30:21.970 --> 00:30:25.490
It certainly made me very self-conscious.

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:29.890
I also had such things as
eggs put into my mailbox.

00:30:31.440 --> 00:30:33.490
Yes, I don't why, hard boiled eggs.

00:30:33.490 --> 00:30:34.580
I guess 'cause I was considered

00:30:34.580 --> 00:30:36.729
to be a hard boiled egg (laughing).

00:30:36.729 --> 00:30:38.041
I don't know what.

00:30:38.041 --> 00:30:39.930
(audience chuckling)

00:30:39.930 --> 00:30:43.080
- I have an absolutely impossible time

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:47.430
imagining my daughter, who
went to Yale College also,

00:30:47.430 --> 00:30:50.447
putting up with any of what
I put up with (laughing).

00:30:52.150 --> 00:30:55.170
- I very much did feel like a pioneer.

00:30:55.170 --> 00:31:00.170
And no one knew what would
happen when we came to Yale.

00:31:01.180 --> 00:31:03.150
- I felt like I was a trailblazer

00:31:03.150 --> 00:31:05.460
and wanted to make this grand experiment

00:31:05.460 --> 00:31:07.070
of co-education that could've gone

00:31:07.070 --> 00:31:09.980
severely off the rails but did not.

00:31:09.980 --> 00:31:14.810
- It planted those seeds
of, of wanting to serve,

00:31:14.810 --> 00:31:18.060
or wanting to be of use to other people.

00:31:18.060 --> 00:31:20.210
- Essential to my even thinking

00:31:20.210 --> 00:31:22.430
about what I wanted to do in my life.

00:31:27.215 --> 00:31:30.382
(audience applauding)

00:31:39.640 --> 00:31:43.203
- The video shows us some
of what we confronted.

00:31:44.750 --> 00:31:47.870
Part of the challenges that year for women

00:31:47.870 --> 00:31:50.630
interested in the arts was what was

00:31:50.630 --> 00:31:53.360
going to be available for us.

00:31:53.360 --> 00:31:58.360
The Slavs early on formed
themselves as a new group.

00:31:58.440 --> 00:32:00.960
What about the Yale Glee Club?

00:32:00.960 --> 00:32:05.820
Founded in 1861 and a men's organization

00:32:05.820 --> 00:32:09.393
for well over 100 years when we arrived.

00:32:11.077 --> 00:32:13.763
In 1969-70,

00:32:14.680 --> 00:32:17.560
that academic year, there was

00:32:17.560 --> 00:32:22.560
initially still a four-part men's chorus

00:32:23.150 --> 00:32:25.280
and a new women's chorus.

00:32:25.280 --> 00:32:28.410
They toured together at
the end of that year,

00:32:28.410 --> 00:32:31.170
singing separately and together.

00:32:31.170 --> 00:32:33.940
And during that successful tour,

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:37.070
it was decided that
the groups would merge,

00:32:37.070 --> 00:32:40.600
and the Glee Club would go co-ed.

00:32:40.600 --> 00:32:44.780
The transition happened over
the course of that first year,

00:32:44.780 --> 00:32:48.830
and the 1970-71 Yale Glee Club

00:32:48.830 --> 00:32:53.830
was the first officially
co-ed Yale Glee Club.

00:32:54.000 --> 00:32:57.800
The Yale Glee Club of
today is world famous.

00:32:57.800 --> 00:33:01.840
Their performances have
received rave reviews

00:33:01.840 --> 00:33:05.400
in the national press
and the New York Times,

00:33:05.400 --> 00:33:08.630
which has called it one
of the best collegiate

00:33:08.630 --> 00:33:13.230
singing ensembles and one
of the most adventurous.

00:33:13.230 --> 00:33:16.790
The Glee Club, of course,
is a mixed chorus.

00:33:16.790 --> 00:33:19.310
But for this special occasion,

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:24.310
the director has set a song
for a group of nine women,

00:33:25.240 --> 00:33:28.760
who will sing from its
earliest repertoire.

00:33:28.760 --> 00:33:32.420
So, now, from the Beatles,

00:33:32.420 --> 00:33:34.130
women from the Glee Club

00:33:34.130 --> 00:33:37.954
singing Paul McCartney's "Yesterday."

00:33:37.954 --> 00:33:42.204
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:34:07.572 --> 00:34:10.655
(gentle piano music)

00:34:21.342 --> 00:34:25.492
♪ Yesterday ♪

00:34:25.492 --> 00:34:30.492
♪ All my troubles seemed so far away ♪

00:34:32.318 --> 00:34:37.318
♪ Now it looks as though
they're here to stay ♪

00:34:37.949 --> 00:34:41.699
♪ Oh, I believe in yesterday ♪

00:34:45.222 --> 00:34:49.769
♪ Suddenly ♪
♪ Suddenly ♪

00:34:49.769 --> 00:34:54.769
♪ I'm not half the man I used to be ♪

00:34:56.541 --> 00:35:01.541
♪ There's a shadow hanging over me ♪

00:35:01.789 --> 00:35:06.789
♪ Oh, yesterday came suddenly ♪

00:35:08.861 --> 00:35:13.861
♪ Why she had to go ♪

00:35:14.961 --> 00:35:16.865
♪ I don't know ♪

00:35:16.865 --> 00:35:21.865
♪ She wouldn't say ♪

00:35:22.241 --> 00:35:27.241
♪ I said something wrong ♪

00:35:27.977 --> 00:35:32.977
♪ Now I long for yesterday ♪

00:35:35.640 --> 00:35:39.969
♪ Yesterday, yesterday ♪

00:35:39.969 --> 00:35:44.969
♪ Love was such an easy game to play ♪

00:35:46.505 --> 00:35:51.505
♪ Now I need a place to hide away ♪

00:35:52.009 --> 00:35:57.009
♪ Oh, I believe in yesterday ♪

00:35:59.197 --> 00:36:04.197
♪ Why she had to go ♪

00:36:05.024 --> 00:36:07.062
♪ I don't know ♪

00:36:07.062 --> 00:36:12.062
♪ She wouldn't say ♪

00:36:12.415 --> 00:36:17.415
♪ I said something wrong ♪

00:36:18.247 --> 00:36:23.247
♪ Now I long for yesterday ♪

00:36:25.679 --> 00:36:29.944
♪ Yesterday ♪

00:36:29.944 --> 00:36:34.944
♪ Love was such an easy game to play ♪

00:36:36.679 --> 00:36:41.679
♪ Now I need a place to hide away ♪

00:36:42.183 --> 00:36:47.183
♪ Oh, I believe in yesterday ♪

00:36:49.677 --> 00:36:52.927
♪ Mm mm mm mm mm mm mm ♪

00:37:17.413 --> 00:37:20.580
(audience applauding)

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:39.173
- Thank you, Yale Glee Club.

00:37:39.173 --> 00:37:40.980
That was absolutely beautiful.

00:37:40.980 --> 00:37:42.760
And if there are members from the first

00:37:42.760 --> 00:37:44.480
classes of women who
were in the Glee Club,

00:37:44.480 --> 00:37:46.734
please stand and wave to us.

00:37:46.734 --> 00:37:50.984
(audience cheering and applauding)

00:37:59.510 --> 00:38:02.740
Tangled Up in Blue, pronounced T.U.I.B,

00:38:02.740 --> 00:38:05.490
was not a group that existed in our years.

00:38:05.490 --> 00:38:07.300
But although they sing
a varied repertoire,

00:38:07.300 --> 00:38:10.240
they have a distinct
folk '60s and '70s vibe

00:38:10.240 --> 00:38:12.130
and are partial to the folk rock hits

00:38:12.130 --> 00:38:16.080
of Dylan, to the jams of
Crosby, Stills, Nash, and Young,

00:38:16.080 --> 00:38:18.810
and to the sounds of Simon and Garfunkel.

00:38:18.810 --> 00:38:20.750
Today they will sing the lead song

00:38:20.750 --> 00:38:22.917
of the Dylan album of the same name,

00:38:22.917 --> 00:38:24.547
"The Times They are A-Changin'"

00:38:25.460 --> 00:38:28.130
as they certainly were
during our time at Yale.

00:38:28.130 --> 00:38:29.666
Welcome, T.U.I.B.

00:38:29.666 --> 00:38:32.833
(audience applauding)

00:38:58.018 --> 00:39:01.711
(gentle acoustic guitar music)

00:39:01.711 --> 00:39:03.672
♪ Come gather 'round, people ♪

00:39:03.672 --> 00:39:06.561
♪ Wherever you roam ♪

00:39:06.561 --> 00:39:10.892
♪ And admit that the waters
around you have grown ♪

00:39:10.892 --> 00:39:15.785
♪ And accept that soon you'll
be drenched to the bone ♪

00:39:15.785 --> 00:39:19.907
♪ If your time to you is worth savin' ♪

00:39:19.907 --> 00:39:21.653
♪ And you better start swimmin' ♪

00:39:21.653 --> 00:39:23.930
♪ Or you'll sink like a stone ♪

00:39:23.930 --> 00:39:28.930
♪ For the times they are a-changin' ♪

00:39:30.812 --> 00:39:35.812
♪ Come writers and critics
who prophesy with your pen ♪

00:39:35.987 --> 00:39:37.763
♪ And keep your eyes wide ♪

00:39:37.763 --> 00:39:40.050
♪ The chance won't come again ♪

00:39:40.050 --> 00:39:42.085
♪ And don't speak too soon ♪

00:39:42.085 --> 00:39:44.908
♪ For the wheel's still in spin ♪

00:39:44.908 --> 00:39:49.065
♪ And there's no tellin'
who that it's namin' ♪

00:39:49.065 --> 00:39:53.220
♪ For the loser now will be later to win ♪

