00:46:15.100 --> 00:46:19.370
- Welfare moms in New Haven
were having their benefits cut,

00:46:19.370 --> 00:46:23.260
and a bunch of us Yalees
went and protested

00:46:23.260 --> 00:46:25.750
with the welfare moms sitting in at the,

00:46:25.750 --> 00:46:27.790
one of these welfare offices.

00:46:27.790 --> 00:46:29.707
And the police came and said,

00:46:29.707 --> 00:46:33.527
"Those of you who don't wanna
be arrested, please leave.

00:46:33.527 --> 00:46:35.850
"Those of you who do, stay."

00:46:35.850 --> 00:46:38.020
And the welfare moms all stayed.

00:46:38.020 --> 00:46:41.600
All but two of us Yalees left.

00:46:41.600 --> 00:46:43.670
I and another woman stayed.

00:46:43.670 --> 00:46:45.360
We were arrested.

00:46:45.360 --> 00:46:49.250
I was only 17 at the time
so the dean of my college

00:46:49.250 --> 00:46:52.470
had to be my guardian,
and not withstanding

00:46:52.470 --> 00:46:54.610
my liberal parents, they were not happy

00:46:54.610 --> 00:46:56.710
to hear when I called from jail.

00:46:56.710 --> 00:47:00.065
Three weeks in, I did make the
cover of the Yale Daily News.

00:47:00.065 --> 00:47:03.065
(audience laughing)

00:47:11.321 --> 00:47:16.321
- It was a galvanizing time
about what side were you on.

00:47:16.667 --> 00:47:19.550
You know, what did you think about what

00:47:19.550 --> 00:47:21.280
was going on, not only in New Haven

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:25.270
but in the broader
United States community.

00:47:25.270 --> 00:47:30.270
And so, yes, it was a big,
big deal about student unrest.

00:47:43.770 --> 00:47:48.770
- The president suspended
academic expectations

00:47:49.820 --> 00:47:54.010
or something like that so
that students could leave.

00:47:54.010 --> 00:47:56.280
Some students left.

00:47:56.280 --> 00:48:00.280
I stayed, but some
demonstrators were talking

00:48:00.280 --> 00:48:01.900
about shutting down Yale.

00:48:01.900 --> 00:48:06.370
And the university decided
to allow demonstrators

00:48:06.370 --> 00:48:11.210
to camp in the colleges, not in the rooms,

00:48:11.210 --> 00:48:15.040
but in the courtyards and feed them.

00:48:15.040 --> 00:48:17.760
- We were serving food in the courtyards

00:48:17.760 --> 00:48:19.510
to the people who were coming in,

00:48:19.510 --> 00:48:21.980
but it was, you know,
it was fairly basic fare

00:48:21.980 --> 00:48:23.850
that you could cook in huge quantities.

00:48:23.850 --> 00:48:26.650
And anybody who wanted to could come in.

00:48:26.650 --> 00:48:30.320
- Calhoun decided that it,
the thing it could do best

00:48:30.320 --> 00:48:33.350
would be to have a hot
line so that we would,

00:48:33.350 --> 00:48:36.020
dispel the fake news, right, and be

00:48:36.020 --> 00:48:40.370
the source of true news, of real news.

00:48:40.370 --> 00:48:45.360
So, I did my dishing up of
the food to whoever showed up

00:48:46.790 --> 00:48:50.700
and manned the hot line.

00:48:50.700 --> 00:48:52.527
- So, things start
heating up on the campus,

00:48:52.527 --> 00:48:54.537
and my mom in New York kept saying,

00:48:54.537 --> 00:48:56.247
"Well, why don't you just come home?

00:48:56.247 --> 00:48:59.347
"I mean, they're suspending exams.

00:48:59.347 --> 00:49:02.437
"They're, you know, you can
write your papers at home.

00:49:02.437 --> 00:49:03.270
"There are no classes.

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:05.340
"Why don't you please come home?"

00:49:05.340 --> 00:49:09.440
And I really felt like I
didn't want to abandon Yale so.

00:49:09.440 --> 00:49:12.820
They did at Trumbull clean out,

00:49:12.820 --> 00:49:14.980
asked us to leave if we could

00:49:14.980 --> 00:49:16.730
the women's entryway.

00:49:16.730 --> 00:49:19.020
So, again, it's about the women

00:49:19.020 --> 00:49:21.320
because they really felt they didn't know

00:49:21.320 --> 00:49:23.170
if they could sustain security.

00:49:23.170 --> 00:49:25.270
- Suddenly, Yale is a place where there

00:49:25.270 --> 00:49:26.740
were teach-ins on the weekend

00:49:26.740 --> 00:49:28.870
about the atrocities in Vietnam.

00:49:28.870 --> 00:49:30.690
SDS was starting up.

00:49:30.690 --> 00:49:34.200
Black Panthers were
having rallies downtown.

00:49:34.200 --> 00:49:36.140
My own guidance counselor, because I had

00:49:36.140 --> 00:49:39.347
gone to some of these things,
said to me in his office,

00:49:39.347 --> 00:49:41.940
"You people should all
be lined up and shot."

00:49:41.940 --> 00:49:45.360
- You know, there were wiretaps
going on amongst students

00:49:45.360 --> 00:49:48.860
and administrators and professors.

00:49:48.860 --> 00:49:50.580
Oh, yeah, there were people who were,

00:49:50.580 --> 00:49:54.480
who got compensated after the fact

00:49:54.480 --> 00:49:57.190
for having been illegally wiretapped.

00:49:57.190 --> 00:50:00.010
Students and faculty

00:50:01.480 --> 00:50:02.760
and administrators.

00:50:02.760 --> 00:50:05.310
- We had a guy who said he was a reporter

00:50:05.310 --> 00:50:06.740
following us around all the time

00:50:06.740 --> 00:50:08.070
because he wanted the inside story.

00:50:08.070 --> 00:50:12.080
I decided with, you know, not at the time,

00:50:12.080 --> 00:50:14.120
but this guy must have been, you know,

00:50:14.120 --> 00:50:16.370
CIA or something or FBI.

00:50:16.370 --> 00:50:19.223
- I was hanging out also with the,

00:50:19.223 --> 00:50:22.870
one of the sub-groups I
met at that initial event,

00:50:22.870 --> 00:50:25.780
ended up hanging out with
William Sloane Coffin Jr.

00:50:25.780 --> 00:50:30.300
and Phil Zader, his assistant chaplain.

00:50:30.300 --> 00:50:32.920
And we were all hanging out together,

00:50:32.920 --> 00:50:35.200
and it turned out later

00:50:35.200 --> 00:50:39.470
that I got named in a class
action suit and got $1000

00:50:39.470 --> 00:50:42.030
'cause my phone was tapped by the CIA

00:50:42.030 --> 00:50:43.433
and the city of New Haven.

00:50:50.240 --> 00:50:52.980
- There were a lot of desperate groups,

00:50:52.980 --> 00:50:56.880
you know, each of whom
was jockeying for power

00:50:56.880 --> 00:51:00.630
and control over the movement
in a sense and the event.

00:51:00.630 --> 00:51:03.820
And I don't think everything
was perfectly peaceful.

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:05.140
I, there was tear gas.

00:51:05.140 --> 00:51:08.140
- I remember running away
from the New Haven police

00:51:08.140 --> 00:51:11.640
and running into Vanderbilt.

00:51:11.640 --> 00:51:14.360
And I peeked out the window, and I know

00:51:14.360 --> 00:51:17.320
I saw a cop throw a tear gas canister

00:51:17.320 --> 00:51:20.640
over the fence at Vanderbilt.

00:51:20.640 --> 00:51:23.410
And then, my memory is that, you know,

00:51:23.410 --> 00:51:25.210
one of my roommates was in there.

00:51:25.210 --> 00:51:28.240
So, she was like, "What's goin' on here?"

00:51:28.240 --> 00:51:30.460
So, I said, "Well, we'll
get the washcloths,

00:51:30.460 --> 00:51:33.720
the wet washcloths, over
your nose and get down."

00:51:33.720 --> 00:51:37.153
- And it was scary to a
little 17, 18-year-old kid,

00:51:37.153 --> 00:51:39.203
I mean, a sheltered kid from Chevy Chase.

00:51:40.100 --> 00:51:41.290
It was very scary.

00:51:41.290 --> 00:51:46.290
- It was such a stressful time,

00:51:46.640 --> 00:51:48.600
not because of the demonstrators

00:51:48.600 --> 00:51:50.583
but because of the politics.

00:51:51.860 --> 00:51:55.910
President Nixon started bombing Cambodia

00:51:55.910 --> 00:52:00.183
just a couple days before
the May Day demonstration.

00:52:01.290 --> 00:52:06.290
A few days after that were
the Kent State shootings,

00:52:07.240 --> 00:52:10.610
and at least one student was killed.

00:52:10.610 --> 00:52:13.350
- It was the first year of co-education,

00:52:13.350 --> 00:52:17.640
the first year of Bobby
Seale going on murder trial,

00:52:17.640 --> 00:52:19.280
then the Kent State killings.

00:52:19.280 --> 00:52:21.740
It was really enough to make any

00:52:21.740 --> 00:52:25.220
middle-class girl's head
spin around 10 or 12 times.

00:52:25.220 --> 00:52:28.223
- So, this is Elga
Wasserman's exact words.

00:52:29.077 --> 00:52:32.107
"The media threat, the media treated

00:52:32.107 --> 00:52:35.767
"the advent of women as
a weird and novel event,

00:52:35.767 --> 00:52:39.277
"as if creatures from
Mars had invaded Yale.

00:52:39.277 --> 00:52:42.147
"In the beginning, few women felt

00:52:42.147 --> 00:52:44.007
"as if they were truly belonged

00:52:44.007 --> 00:52:47.677
"in this bastion of future male leaders.

00:52:47.677 --> 00:52:50.847
"Oddly enough, from my perspective,

00:52:50.847 --> 00:52:54.267
"May Day 1970 became the best thing

00:52:54.267 --> 00:52:57.347
"that happened to Yale co-education.

00:52:57.347 --> 00:53:01.617
"The external threat of May
Day unified the student body.

00:53:01.617 --> 00:53:03.907
"Everyone pitched in together.

00:53:03.907 --> 00:53:06.527
"Suddenly, gender was irrelevant,

00:53:06.527 --> 00:53:08.737
"and undergraduate women had become

00:53:08.737 --> 00:53:11.277
"an integral part of Yale College."

00:53:12.530 --> 00:53:14.190
From Elga Wasserman.

00:53:14.190 --> 00:53:17.227
- You know, I think that
for many in my generation

00:53:17.227 --> 00:53:21.000
who were, who lived here then,

00:53:21.000 --> 00:53:25.910
our, our moral compass was formed

00:53:26.890 --> 00:53:31.590
by being students here, experiencing that,

00:53:32.610 --> 00:53:37.610
and having that leadership
of those folks at that time.

00:53:40.622 --> 00:53:43.789
(audience applauding)

00:53:51.070 --> 00:53:55.070
At that moment, the spring of 1970,

00:53:55.070 --> 00:53:59.413
we really did the earth
move under our feet.

00:54:01.400 --> 00:54:05.700
To sing the Carole King song by that name

00:54:05.700 --> 00:54:07.973
for us is Whim 'N Rhythm.

00:54:08.950 --> 00:54:12.900
In 1981 Yale's a cappella scene

00:54:12.900 --> 00:54:16.220
featured a number of single sex groups,

00:54:16.220 --> 00:54:19.930
but that year seven Senior women felt

00:54:19.930 --> 00:54:24.000
that a key part of the Yale musical scene

00:54:24.000 --> 00:54:27.460
was missing a Senior
group that would rival

00:54:27.460 --> 00:54:31.700
the decades old tradition
of the Whiffenpoofs.

00:54:31.700 --> 00:54:36.260
Nearly 40 years later, the
singers of Whim 'N Rhythm

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:40.110
represent the best and most experienced

00:54:40.110 --> 00:54:43.450
women singers in the Senior class.

00:54:43.450 --> 00:54:47.892
Here they are singing
"I Feel the Earth Move."

